    "evidence_text": "Pharmacologic characterization of a potent inhibitor of class I phosphatidylinositide 3-kinases.\n\nPI3K inhibitors thus have considerable potential as molecular cancer therapeutics.\n\nPI103 was extensively metabolized, but distributed rapidly to tissues and tumors.\n\nThis resulted in tumor growth delay in eight different human cancer xenograft models with various PI3K pathway abnormalities.\n\nDecreased phosphorylation of AKT was observed in U87MG gliomas, consistent with drug levels achieved.\n\nInhibitors of PtdIns 3-kinase could potentially give a better understanding of the function and regulatory mechanisms of the enzyme.\n\nChanges in the levels of inositol phosphates after agonist-dependent hydrolysis of membrane phosphoinositides.\n\nThe tissues were first incubated with [3H]inositol, which was incorporated into the phosphoinositides.\n\nThere was no evidence for the existence of inositol 1:2-cyclic phosphate.\n\nA simple anion-exchange chromatographic method was developed for separating these inositol phosphates for quantitative analysis.\n\nPTEN Regulates PI(3,4)P2 Signaling Downstream of Class I PI3K\n\nThe PI3K signaling pathway regulates cell growth and movement and is heavily mutated in cancer.\n\nClass I PI3Ks synthesize the lipid messenger PI(3,4,5)P3.\n\nThe PTEN tumor suppressor is thought to function primarily as a PI(3,4,5)P3 3-phosphatase, limiting activation of this pathway.\n\nHere we show that PTEN also functions as a PI(3,4)P2 3-phosphatase, both in vitro and in vivo.\n\nThese results point to a role for PI(3,4)P2 in the phenotype caused by loss-of-function mutations or deletions in PTEN.\n\nThese data have important predictive and therapeutic clinical implications.\n\nNuclear PTEN functions as an essential regulator of SRF-dependent transcription to control smooth muscle differentiation\n\nPhosphoinositide 3-kinase signalling--which way to target?\n\nOther downstream effectors link PI3K to cell motility and the control of cardiovascular parameters.\n\nHowever, PI3Ks also modulate vital processes such as metabolic control and nutrient uptake.\n\nThe isolation and characterization of a cDNA encoding phospholipid-specific inositol polyphosphate 5-phosphatase.\n\nAll previously described members hydrolyze water soluble inositol phosphates.\n\nWe designate this 5-phosphatase as type IV.\n\nmRNA was detected in many tissues and cell lines as determined by Northern blotting.",
